{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"marketplace-expansion-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Marketplace expansion track","description":"Takes an online shop onto a new marketplace step by step - fit and fees, which products to list, listings, stock and fulfilment setup, then a launch review - pausing for approval after each step.","category":"ecommerce","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","operate","review"],"role":["founder","operations-manager","marketer"],"subject":["ecommerce","retail"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["preferences","dataset","notes"],"output":["plan","table","copy","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["new-sales-channel","marketplace-selling","channel-expansion","product-selection","listing-launch","seller-metrics"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-marketplace-listing","plan-marketplace-ads","plan-multichannel-inventory-sync","calculate-product-margin"],"personas":["online-store-manager"]},"args":[{"name":"marketplace","description":"The marketplace you are considering, e.g. a general marketplace, a handmade marketplace, a regional one, and the country of that marketplace.","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"shop_profile","description":"What you sell, number of products, price points and margins, current channels and monthly orders, how you fulfil today, and your team's time.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"fit-and-fees","file":"steps/01-fit-and-fees.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"product-selection","file":"steps/02-product-selection.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"listings","file":"steps/03-listings.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"operations-setup","file":"steps/04-operations-setup.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"launch-review","file":"steps/05-launch-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Takes **{{shop_profile}}** onto **{{marketplace}}** in five steps: decide whether the marketplace fits and what it really costs, choose the products to start with, write the listings, set up stock, fulfilment and customer service so the shop can meet the marketplace's performance standards, then review the first weeks and decide whether to expand, adjust or stop. Each step produces one deliverable and stops for the owner's approval; later steps use the approved decisions (the chosen products, prices and fulfilment method) without drifting. If the owner asks to skip approvals, say in one sentence that each step depends on the one before, and continue only once they confirm. Marketplace fees, category rules, listing limits and performance thresholds change often and differ by country: the assistant never states them as facts, marks every one as \"confirm in the marketplace's seller help pages\", and uses placeholders until the owner supplies them. Nothing is invented about the shop's numbers or products.","steps":[{"id":"fit-and-fees","file":"steps/01-fit-and-fees.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Fit and fees\n\nDecide whether {{marketplace}} is worth it for this shop.\n\n1. If essential facts are missing (margins, average order value, current monthly orders, how orders are fulfilled), ask for them in one short message, or state assumptions and continue.\n2. Fit: who shops on this marketplace and why, how the shop's products and prices compare with what sells there, how much control the shop gives up (customer data, branding, pricing rules, returns policy), and the dependence risk if it grows to a large share of sales.\n3. Costs to confirm: list every cost type (account or subscription fee, referral or commission fee, payment fee, fulfilment fee if using the marketplace's fulfilment, advertising usually needed for visibility, returns) with a placeholder for each figure and where to find it.\n4. Contribution per order: using the shop's own price and cost per product and the placeholders, show the contribution per order on the marketplace next to the same order on the shop's own store.\n5. Verdict: go, go with conditions, or not now, with the reason and the two or three facts that would change it.\n\nStop and wait for approval or edits. Do not choose products yet."},{"id":"product-selection","file":"steps/02-product-selection.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Product selection\n\nChoose the products to launch with, based on the approved fit and fees.\n\n1. Score each product or product group on: contribution after marketplace costs, demand and competition on the marketplace (from what the owner can see or provide), shipping size and weight, return risk, restricted-category or approval needs (to confirm), and stock depth.\n2. Recommend a launch set of about 5 to 20 products, usually the strongest margin items with proven sales elsewhere, and say which to hold back and why (low margin after fees, fragile, one-offs that risk overselling).\n3. Set marketplace prices: whether to match own-store prices or adjust for fees, noting that some marketplaces have price-parity expectations to confirm.\n4. List what each launch product still needs: identifiers such as barcodes if required, extra photos, compliance documents, variant structure.\n\nStop and wait for approval or edits. Do not write listings yet."},{"id":"listings","file":"steps/03-listings.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Listings\n\nWrite listings for the approved launch products.\n\n1. For each product: a title following the marketplace's usual pattern (brand, product type, key attribute, size or quantity), bullet points or key features led by what buyers search and care about, a description, the attributes to fill, and search terms or tags. Keep within limits the owner gives; otherwise mark limits as \"confirm\".\n2. Use only facts from the shop's product data. Mark unknown specs as [CONFIRM: ...]. No claims the shop cannot back up, no competitor brand names as keywords.\n3. A photo checklist per product: main image on the background the marketplace expects (to confirm), scale, detail, in use, and any infographic.\n4. If there are more than about ten products, fully write the first three, then give a template and the specific inputs for the rest.\n\nStop and wait for approval or edits. Do not set up operations yet."},{"id":"operations-setup","file":"steps/04-operations-setup.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Operations setup\n\nMake sure the shop can meet the marketplace's service standards from day one.\n\n1. Stock: how stock for the approved products will sync with the shop's other channels, the source of truth, and buffers for low-stock items to avoid cancellations.\n2. Fulfilment: self-fulfilled or marketplace fulfilment, the handling time to set, carrier and tracking requirements to confirm, packaging, and the daily routine for marketplace orders.\n3. Customer service and returns: response time the shop can keep, message templates for common questions, the returns process and how it fits the marketplace's return rules (to confirm).\n4. Metrics to watch: the seller performance measures this marketplace uses (to confirm by name and threshold), who checks them, and how often.\n5. Launch checklist: account verification, tax and business details, payout account, listings live, test order if possible, first-week advertising budget cap if ads are planned.\n\nStop and wait for approval or edits."},{"id":"launch-review","file":"steps/05-launch-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Launch review\n\nReview the first four to eight weeks when the owner shares results.\n\n1. Ask for the results if not provided: orders, sessions or impressions, conversion rate, advertising spend and sales, fees paid, returns, cancellations, late shipments, customer messages, and performance metrics.\n2. Compare actual contribution per order with the step 1 estimate, and the time spent with what the team can sustain.\n3. Diagnose: which products work, which do not, and why (visibility, price, listing quality, reviews, delivery speed).\n4. Decide: expand the range, adjust (price, listings, advertising, fulfilment), or stop, with the next three actions and the date of the next review.\n5. Finish with a one-page summary of decisions made through all five steps."}]}
